<%# Plausible, and whatever the last request wants counted. See app/services/analytics.rb
    for what is measured and what is refused, and concerns/tracked_events.rb for
    how an event gets from a controller to here.

    The stub on the second line is Plausible's own: it defines window.plausible
    synchronously so anything that fires before the async script has landed is
    queued and replayed rather than dropped. That is what lets lib/analytics.js
    send an event on turbo:load without knowing or caring whether the script is
    down yet.

    Turbo keeps this working across visits without any help. It copies script
    elements it has not seen before and leaves the ones it has, so the two tags
    below load exactly once per full page load and are not re-run on a Turbo
    visit — which is right, because a second plausible.init() would be a second
    set of history listeners and every pageview would be counted twice. %>
<% if Analytics.enabled?(current_user) %>
  <script async src="<%= Analytics.script_url %>"></script>
  <script>
    window.plausible=window.plausible||function(){(plausible.q=plausible.q||[]).push(arguments)},plausible.init=plausible.init||function(i){plausible.o=i||{}};
    plausible.init({ transformRequest: <%= Analytics.path_normalizer.html_safe %> })
  </script>
<% end %>

<%# The event itself is a meta tag rather than a third <script>, for the other
    half of that Turbo rule: a meta tag is "provisional", so Turbo removes the
    old one and copies the new one on every visit, while a script would be
    appended and left there — one more dead tag in <head> for every page the
    student opens. lib/analytics.js reads this on turbo:load.

    Rendered whether or not the script above is — with no plausible on the page
    it is an inert tag nobody reads, and it means the events can be tested for
    without a spec having to pretend it is running in production. %>
<% if (event = tracked_event) %>
  <meta name="analytics-event" content="<%= event.to_json %>">
<% end %>
